<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/a3e2e732-fcf9-48b3-9f2c-ed2903975fc4> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "el++ rules return a firing flag and a fixed head" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/a3e2e732-fcf9-48b3-9f2c-ed2903975fc4> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/a3e2e732-fcf9-48b3-9f2c-ed2903975fc4> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "Each [Krö10] Fig. 3 rule instance in src/rules/ksc.slop is a @pure, loop-free function over exactly the rule's premises, written as their fields in the rule's order (EDB axiom first). It returns `Concl {fires Bool, fact KFact}`: `fact` is the rule's head over these premises, built unconditionally, and `fires` says whether the body matches (joins and nom tests), computed with nested `if`s. Three proved @propertys per rule read as the Datalog rule: `sound` (fires only if the body matches), `complete` (fires whenever it does) and `head` (fact is the head over the premises' own terms, the K2 provenance condition). Nested terms in a head are built through constructor helpers in types.slop (`name-term`, `ind-elem`, `aux-elem`, `class-elem`, `term-elem`), which the contracts call too.\n\nWhy this shape, all found by probing slop main 28a6270:\n- An `(Option KFact)` result: the verifier does not link the payload of `some` around a nested constructor, so the head was unprovable.\n- `(union-new KTerm k-name z)` nested inside the head, or a head chosen by a `match` in the body: the verifier does not model a nested single-payload variant's payload, and a match-bound value loses field projection. Helper calls are uninterpreted but identical in body and contract, so they compare.\n- `(not (. $result fires))` does not translate; `(== (. $result fires) false)` does.\n- A `(List KFact)` result like el's rules: an allocation per rule call on the hot path, and the nested-constructor problem remains.\n\nEvidence: 28 rule functions verify (sound/complete/head; (1), (*) and (3) always fire). 108 mutants (forcing fires true/false, dropping each join condition, swapping a head term) are each killed by exactly the contract they target, with no collateral failure. The rule-instance table in test.slop runs one firing and one non-firing case per rule (slop's @example compares a record result with C ==, so they cannot be @examples)." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
