<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/ca12fa7d-7047-4db0-84ca-cef7d298a563> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#ArchitecturalDecision> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/ca12fa7d-7047-4db0-84ca-cef7d298a563> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Decode reads HOWL's own triple store, not slop-rdf's IndexedGraph" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/ca12fa7d-7047-4db0-84ca-cef7d298a563> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/ca12fa7d-7047-4db0-84ca-cef7d298a563> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "`src/termstore.slop` (M1 slice 6b step B) replaces slop-rdf's `IndexedGraph` in decode. It answers exactly the questions decode asks:\n- `store-objects (s, p, ?)`\n- `store-typed (?, rdf:type, T)`\n- `store-contains (s, p, o)`\n- `store-has-any (s, p)`\n\n**Structure.**\n- **Term table.** It gives each term an Int id by content: IRIs, blank nodes and literals through a `(Map Term Int)`, whose generated hash/eq is content-based since slop #205; quoted triples through a structural key built from their parts' ids.\n- **SPO index.** It maps an `(s, p)` id pair to a Set of objects. Duplicate detection is folded into the insert.\n- **rdf:type index.** It maps a type to a Set of subjects, and replaces POS, because decode's only subjects query is `(?, rdf:type, T)`. The API has that shape, so nothing can ask another question.\n- **slop-rdf still provides** parsing, Term/Triple/IRI, and vocabulary.\n\n**Why.** On GO, slop-rdf's four-index build (SPO/PSO/OSP/POS, keyed by whole Terms, plus a `contains` check per insert) was 35–40% of the whole run and ~80% of the front end. HOWL read only two of the indexes.\n\n**Measured** (bench/results.txt):\n- **GO:** 9.52× → 7.16× ELK, classify 4.25 → 3.99 s; in side-by-side runs, prepare ~4.5 → ~2.6 s; peak memory 19.8 → 13.9 GB.\n- **OBI:** 0.68× → 0.52× HermiT; peak 2.3 → 1.7 GB.\n- **EL-GALEN:** its front end is small, so the bench moved only with load.\n\n**Semantics.** Every report is unchanged: goldens, all corpus report hashes, and determinism on fixtures and corpus. There is one deliberate change, unreachable from any file: quoted triples are one term by structure, matching `term-eq`, where IndexedGraph keyed them by pointer. slop-rdf's Turtle parser rejects `<< >>`, so only a library caller building triples directly can supply one.\n\n**Verification:**\n- Tests 91/0: `test-term-store-by-content` covers literals keyed by content through a runtime-built datatype string, duplicates, the type index and unseen terms; `test-term-store-quoted-and-tagged` covers quoted triples, nested ones, and language tags.\n- Mutations, each seen red: literals minted per occurrence; `contains` never true; the type index never written; quoted triples minted per occurrence.\n- Codex review: the quoted-triple change was its one finding, taken as deliberate and documented.\n\n**Found along the way (slop):** `set-len` on a Set fetched by `map-get` from a Map of Sets does not compile. The value is `void*`, and `set-len` lowers to `(x)->len` with no `slop_map*` cast. `store-has-any` doesn't need a count: an `(s, p)` entry exists exactly when it has an object." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
