<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/efa41206-ca53-4fa3-80f9-505ac464bddc> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Seed the delta — and the round-0 snapshot, which is not the same thing" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/efa41206-ca53-4fa3-80f9-505ac464bddc> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/efa41206-ca53-4fa3-80f9-505ac464bddc> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "In a semi-naive fixpoint, initial facts must reach BOTH the initial delta and the round-0 snapshot the driver joins against. Writing them only to the store leaves every work queue empty; writing them only to the queue leaves the snapshot empty. Each mistake is silent and each costs different conclusions.\n\nTHE TWO STATES ARE DIFFERENT, and conflating them is the whole trap. S₋₁ is genuinely empty. The state round 0 READS is S₀ = S₋₁ ∪ Δ₀ = Δ₀. A driver that commits Δ at the preceding barrier — the shape HOWL uses, so that no round ever writes the store — makes initialization that barrier, so the seeds go into `subsumers` AND into `queues`.\n\nFAILURE MODE A, store-only (the original finding): rules fire only on facts arriving in Δₙ, and `active` tracks contexts with non-empty queues, so `active` is empty at round 0. The driver reports `fixpoint` having fired nothing. This is especially dangerous because the run terminates instantly, reports fixpoint with empty coverage omissions — i.e. COMPLETE — and returns an empty classification, which the verdict rule reads as a clean \"coherent\". Sound, catastrophically incomplete, indistinguishable from success at every interface.\n\nFAILURE MODE B, queue-only: S(N) is empty for the whole of round 0, so any rule whose premise is a SEED cannot fire. CR2 never finds the other conjunct of a `sub-and`; CR4 never satisfies B ∈ S(Y) against a seed. In HOWL this loses precisely the §3.1 litmus, because CR4 there joins the arriving edge against `Person ∈ S(Person)` — a seed. Verified by mutation: deleting the two store writes fails the litmus test and NOTHING else, so a suite without that fixture goes green on a materializer.\n\nWHY BOTH ARE NEARLY UNTESTABLE BY ACCIDENT. Any fixture with ordinary axioms generates deltas through other paths and masks mode A. Mode B needs a fixture whose derivation genuinely depends on a seed as a rule premise, which most hand-written examples do not. The tests that catch them must have the seed as their ONLY enabling premise — e.g. `⊤ ⊑ C` over an otherwise empty ontology for mode A, the litmus for mode B.\n\nCaptured 2026-08-13, corrected 2026-08-19 against the working driver." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/efa41206-ca53-4fa3-80f9-505ac464bddc> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
