<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/f3bcf088-7d14-4136-8c29-8790b8a793e7> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/f3bcf088-7d14-4136-8c29-8790b8a793e7> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The slop verify boundary, as of 0.2.3 with #168 and #172" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/f3bcf088-7d14-4136-8c29-8790b8a793e7> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/f3bcf088-7d14-4136-8c29-8790b8a793e7> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "What `slop verify` can prove about HOWL's code is set by how the verifier models a body, not by what the claim means. The status below is for slop 0.2.3 plus slop-lang/slop PRs #168 and #172 (#166, #170), re-probed 2026-09.\n\nNOW PROVABLE:\n1. Quantified claims over a LOOP-FREE push-built result. A body `(let ((mut r (list-new …))) … r)`, with pushes only inside do/let/when/if/cond/match, is modelled exactly as a sequence. So `forall`, `exists` and `list-contains` over `$result` are proved or refuted. On 0.2.3 alone soundness came back unknown and completeness FAILED: `list-push` was an opaque call, and `_seq_push` had never been wired in. The #69 behaviour (fails, and poisons a sibling @post) is gone since PR #126.\n2. `match` in a contract binds every payload, at its declared sort (#166). Before, only the first name was bound, later names read another arm's payload, and false contracts verified.\n3. Inside that exact model, a callee's `@post`/`@property` is assumed at the call, guarded by the path condition and the callee's `@pre`.\n4. `@example` EXECUTES (fixed in 0.2.1, #71). Trap: a list-returning example's expected value is SPLAT as literal elements, `(list <elem> …)` with no type argument. `(list Addressed)` makes the harness treat the type name as an element.\n5. Multi-payload union equality (#66) and `set-remove` chain-stranding (#67) are fixed.\n\nSTILL NOT:\n6. `or`/`and` in a BODY make the return value opaque. `(if (. r b) true …)` verifies where `(or (. r b) …)` fails. The boolean field is fine; the operator is the blocker.\n7. A LOOP anywhere in the body makes `$result` opaque. The exact model doesn't follow loops, so HOWL's four loop rules stay owed. Hoist a loop into a helper, or carry facts across it with `@loop-invariant`.\n8. Two exclusive branches that each push defeat a `<= 1` bound. Set a flag and push ONCE.\n9. No interprocedural reasoning outside the exact model: a callee is uninterpreted, apart from its instantiated posts. Where a property must cross a call, add a redundant local check.\n10. `const` is opaque. `set-elements`/`list-get` are uninterpreted. `(Int 0 ..)` fields are interpreted.\n11. Opaque predicates. `node-eq`, `role-eq` and `set-has` are uninterpreted: equal inputs aren't known to give equal answers, and `node-eq(b,a1) ∧ node-eq(b,a2)` doesn't give `a1 = a2`. A contract states premises with the body's own calls, and a self-join's second side states the body's case split.\n12. Timeouts are an honest outcome. Some true contracts don't decide in reasonable time; CR2's completeness as one disjunction didn't decide at 120 s, but the body's split did at the normal timeout. A false claim often comes back unknown (timeout) rather than failed. That is sound: it never verifies.\n13. `record-new`/`union-new` inside a contract is a fresh value that nothing equals. Write completeness as `exists` over field equalities.\n14. The transpiler lowers a multi-payload `match` in expression position wrongly (#171), breaking a `SLOP_DEBUG` build of such a `@post`. State those facts as `@property`, which is verify-only.\n\nTHE METHOD:\n- Minimal pairs, and mutate the body to see a proved property go red.\n- Don't diagnose a limitation from one failing example, and don't diagnose a FIX from a closed issue.\n- Before citing an upstream issue as a blocker, check its current state. #69 had been closed three weeks when S2 planning cited it, and the user caught it.\n- Codex review rounds of the verifier change found eleven false-verification paths across six rounds (two on #168, four on #172). Three were already wrong on main: tail record fields under a shadowing `let`, quoted enum arms, and `Float` payloads." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/f3bcf088-7d14-4136-8c29-8790b8a793e7> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/f3bcf088-7d14-4136-8c29-8790b8a793e7> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasRationale> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Rationale/6c8c210f-80f6-4373-8ed7-238a29908fd8> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
